---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { AUTHORS } from '../config/authors';
import { CATEGORIES } from '../config/categories';
import { SITE } from '../config/site.config';
import { getPublishedArticles } from '../lib/articles';
import ArticleCTA from '../components/ArticleCTA.astro';

const articles = await getPublishedArticles();
---

<PageLayout
  title={`About ${SITE.name}`}
  description={`${SITE.name} publishes independent AI news, hands-on tutorials, tool reviews and model comparisons for people who actually build with AI.`}
  lede="Independent AI coverage for people who build with the tools, not just read about them."
>
  <p>
    Most AI coverage splits into two piles: launch posts rewritten from a press release, and
    threads promising that everything changed this week. {SITE.name} is written for the
    people in between — engineers, technical founders and operators who have to decide which
    model to call, which agent framework to commit to, and whether a tool is worth the
    migration cost.
  </p>

  <p>
    We publish across {CATEGORIES.length} topic areas, with {articles.length}
    {articles.length === 1 ? 'article' : 'articles'} in the archive. The remit is AI news,
    AI tools, AI agents, LLMs, automation, AI coding, prompt engineering and the tutorials
    that tie them together.
  </p>

  <h2>What we publish</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/news/">News</a></strong> — model launches, lab research, funding and
      policy, written with enough technical detail to tell a capability shift from a
      marketing cycle.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/tutorials/">Tutorials</a></strong> — step-by-step builds with LLM
      APIs, agents, RAG pipelines, MCP servers and AI coding assistants. Working code, run
      before it is published.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/reviews/">Reviews</a></strong> — hands-on evaluations of AI tools
      and platforms, including the parts that do not work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/comparisons/">Comparisons</a></strong> — head-to-head assessments of
      models, assistants and platforms, decided on evidence and on what each one is actually
      for.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/articles/">Analysis</a></strong> — longer explainers on how these
      systems work underneath, which is the half of the subject that stays true longer than
      a version number.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Alongside the writing we maintain three reference surfaces: the
    <a href="/tools/">AI tool directory</a>, the <a href="/agents/">AI agent directory</a>
    and the <a href="/prompts/">prompt library</a>. There is also a
    <a href="/resources/">resources</a> section for the papers, docs and repos we keep
    returning to.
  </p>

  <h2>How we approach accuracy</h2>
  <p>
    AI moves fast enough that confident invention is the default failure mode of the
    category. We do not publish benchmark scores, pricing figures or context-window numbers
    we cannot verify against a primary source. Where a claim is genuinely uncertain or
    likely to change within a month, we say so in the text rather than rounding it into a
    fact. The full standard is set out in the
    <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Editorial independence</h2>
  <p>
    We do not sell coverage. No vendor pays to appear in the tool or agent directory, no
    vendor pays for a higher rating, and no vendor sees an article before it publishes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site is funded by reader support, contextual advertising and affiliate links on some
    outbound tool links. Affiliate arrangements never determine whether a tool is covered,
    how it is rated, or what the review says about its weaknesses. Where a commercial
    relationship is relevant to a piece, it is disclosed in that piece. Our position on all
    of this is spelled out in the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Editorial desks</h2>
  <p>
    Articles are attributed to the desk that produced them. Each desk owns accuracy and
    technical review inside its area.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {
      AUTHORS.map((author) => (
        <div class="surface p-5">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <span
              class="grid size-10 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full text-sm font-bold text-white"
              style={`background:${author.accent}`}
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              {author.initials}
            </span>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-bold text-[var(--text)]">
                <a href={`/authors/${author.slug}/`} class="hover:text-[var(--color-primary)]">
                  {author.name}
                </a>
              </p>
              <p class="text-xs text-[var(--text-subtle)]">{author.role}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--text-muted)]">{author.bio}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If something here is wrong, tell us. Corrections are made as soon as they are confirmed
    and, where the change is material, noted on the article with a date. Send the article
    URL and the specific claim you are disputing to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.organization.email}`}>{SITE.organization.email}</a>, or use the
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Follow along</h2>
  <p>
    Every new article goes to the <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a> as soon as it publishes.
    The AIInsider Briefing is a low-volume email covering new models, tools worth your time
    and what actually shipped. You can also browse by
    <a href="/categories/">category</a> or <a href="/tags/">topic</a>, or use
    <a href="/search/">search</a> if you know what you are looking for.
  </p>

  <ArticleCTA title="About" path={Astro.url.pathname} class="mt-16" />
</PageLayout>
